        'Every switchgear panel has a nameplate: a metal plate with its ratings, the values the manufacturer guarantees and has proven in tests under the IEC 62271 series of standards.\n\nThe rated voltage is the highest network voltage the equipment is built for. It is a standard step above the nominal network voltage: 12 kV for a 10 kV network, 24 kV for 20 kV. The rated current is the current it can carry continuously without overheating, often 400, 630 or 1,250 A.',
        'Then the fault values. The short-time withstand current, with its duration, is the fault current the panel can carry while closed without damage: for example 20 kA for 1 s, the standard duration.\n\nThe peak withstand current covers the first peak of a fault, which can be much higher than the steady fault current; the magnetic forces then try to bend busbars and blow contacts open. At 50 Hz the standard sets it at 2.5 times the short-time withstand current: 50 kA for a 20 kA panel.',
        'A circuit breaker has two more. Its breaking capacity is the largest short-circuit current it can interrupt, usually equal to the short-time value. Its making capacity is the peak current it can close onto without welding its contacts, again 2.5 times.\n\nA load-break switch has a making capacity, because someone may close it onto a fault, but no breaking capacity for faults. That is the make-and-break question from the first lesson, now in numbers on a plate.',
        'The rule is simple: every rating must be at least the duty at that spot. The grid operator calculates the fault level at each busbar and at each customer connection. Large customers get that value from the operator when they design or extend their own MV installation.\n\nThe plate often also shows the internal arc classification, for example IAC AFL 20 kA 1 s: tested for an arc inside the panel, safe for authorised people at the front and sides.',
        'In practice you read nameplates on panel doors, in tender specifications and in the asset register, the database of all equipment. Typical failure: the ratings were checked once, at installation, and the fault level grew afterwards, through a bigger transformer or a new generator nearby.\n\nExperts argue how much margin to buy: a 25 kA panel costs more, but survives decades of growth. A sharp question: “What fault level was this panel chosen for, and what is it today?”',
