        kind: 'faded',
        text: 'At a 150/10 kV substation, a feeder fault is cleared not by the feeder breaker but after 1.1 s by the transformer incomer: 14 feeders and 9,000 customers are out for 40 minutes. The event log shows a “DC fault” alarm for the station battery that started three weeks earlier and was acknowledged daily in the control room.',
        ask: 'What probably happened, and what do you ask?',
        reveal: 'Likely the battery or its supply was too weak to trip the feeder breaker, so the backup protection on the incomer cleared the fault, later and wider: selectivity lost through the DC system, not the settings.\n\nAsk: why was a DC alarm acknowledged for three weeks without a work order? Who owns DC alarms, and what priority do they get? Are other stations showing the same alarm now? Is trip circuit supervision fitted and connected here? And how did the event count in the outage figures: 9,000 customers × 40 minutes is 360,000 customer minutes from one quiet alarm.',
